const e="kerala-school-sasthrolsavam-social-science-fair",l=2025,t="Read in full from the Kerala School Sasthrolsavam Manual 2025 (Malayalam; 250 PDF pages): Chapters 1-5 (common rules, levels, committees, appeals, championship), Chapter 8 (Social Science Fair) and the entry forms and annexures. The manual is in a legacy font, so every page was read from its image; page numbers in Source are the printed numbers. The manual prints no dates for the 2025 festival, no venue or address, no phone numbers or e-mail, no website for the online entry, no fixed last date to register, and no cash amount for prizes. These are not captured. The only dated item is the order of 12 September 2025 (a news Variable). Amounts printed (appeal fees, registration fee at the sub-district fair, fee for a new certificate) are Variables. The manual prints no score table for Social Science Quiz, Talent Search Examination and the Teaching Aid. The History Seminar marks (40, 40, 10) add up to 90; the manual also prints 10 marks for the seminar aids. The Teaching Aid for social science teachers is not in the template (teachers only) and is named in one item under Other Parts of the Manual. The Kerala School Skill Festival and the other fairs of the manual are named in one line under Other Parts of the Manual.",a=JSON.parse('{"Title":"Rules & Criteria","Subtitle":"Kerala School Sasthrolsavam, Social Science Fair. Read these rules before you register.","NoticeTitle":"Please Read Before Registering","Notice":"The Social Science Fair is held for LP, UP, HS and HSS/VHSS children at the school, sub-district, revenue district and state levels. The head master or principal registers the entries online.","Notes":["A child can take part in only one item of one fair, and in the quiz of one field.","A school sends to the next level only the first place winners with an A grade at the school fair.","Make the entries with the photo of every child.","Write the Malayalam items (Elocution, Local History Writing, News Reading) in Malayalam.","A complaint about a result must be given within one hour after the result is announced."],"Sections":[{"Key":"general","Title":"General Rules","Items":["The Kerala School Sasthrolsavam is the Science, Mathematics, Social Science, Work Experience and IT fair of the children in LP, UP, HS, Higher Secondary and Vocational Higher Secondary classes of the Government, Aided and Unaided (recognised) schools of Kerala under the General Education Department.","All earlier Science, Maths, Social Science, Work Experience and IT fair manuals and amendments are cancelled by this manual.","The competitions are held from school level to state level on the dates in the education calendar that the department publishes each year, or on the dates the Director of General Education gives.","The fair of each school is held by the clubs of the school. The fairs of the sub-district and revenue district are held by the club associations or councils of that level. The state fair is held by the department and the state club associations or councils.","The Director of General Education gives the directions needed to run the Kerala School Sasthrolsavam.","The fairs are held on the subject that is told from time to time under the rules and directions of the General Education Department.","Aim: to grow scientific, mathematical, social science, work experience and IT interest and research interest in the children, and to grow what they learn in a way that helps the society they live in.","Aim: to train the children to use well in daily life the knowledge they gain from science and technology, mathematics, social science, work experience and IT.","Aim: to help the children know the link between people and their surroundings and understand the method of gaining this knowledge.","Aim: to grow the inquiry mind and the research mind along with the gain of knowledge.","Aim: to find and nurture the natural abilities of the children.","Aim: to understand current events against the background of past and lasting history, and to keep them for the future generation in a useful way.","Aim: to introduce the importance and use of free software.","Aim: to give chances to know new technical fields in IT and use them, to do creative work with digital media, and to practise a computer language.","Aim: to make the children able to win their own scientific and technical achievements in the future, and to gain practical skill in science, maths, social science, work experience and IT.","The topics for the Social Science Fair are given each year by the State Social Science Clubs Association and the schools are told in advance."]},{"Key":"eligibility","Title":"Eligibility","Items":["Children of the Government, Aided and Unaided (recognised) schools under the General Education Department of Kerala can take part.","The competition is held in four categories by class. Category I is class 1 to 4 (LP). Category II is class 5 to 7 (UP). Category III is class 8 to 10 (HS). Category IV is class 11 and 12 (HSS and VHSS).","Category I and II competitions end at the sub-district level. Category III and IV competitions go up to the state level.","A school fair is open to every child of the school. Every child gets the chance to take part by category.","Only a competitor who got first place with an A grade in the item at the school fair can take part in the sub-district competition.","In Category III and IV, only a competitor who got the first or second place with an A grade at the sub-district level can take part in the revenue district competition, and only a competitor who got the first or second place with an A grade at the revenue district level can take part in the state competition.","A child can take part in only one item of one of the five fairs (Science, Maths, Social Science, Work Experience, IT). In addition, the child can take part in the quiz competition of any one field.","A competitor with marks below 60 percent in an item gets no grade. If only one child takes part in an item, the child goes to the sub-district fair only if the performance is good enough for a grade.","If nobody gets first place with an A grade in an item at the school fair, no competitor is sent from the school to the sub-district fair for that item.","If two competitors get equal marks for the first or the second place in an item at the school fair, the competitor for the upper level is chosen by the total marks of the evaluation plus the marks for evaluation criterion (A).","A school can take part at the sub-district level only if it is affiliated to the science, maths, social science, work experience and IT club association or council of that sub-district.","A sub-district can take part at the revenue district level only if it is affiliated to the club association or council of that revenue district.","All district level clubs of a revenue district must be affiliated to the state club association or council before the revenue district takes part in the state fair.","At the sub-district fair, the competitor brings the school level certificate that the head master or principal of the school has attested.","At the revenue district fair, the competitor brings the certificate of recognition from the head master or principal of the school, attested by the sub-district education officer.","At the state fair, the competitor brings the certificate of recognition from the head master or principal of the school, attested by the Deputy Director of Education.","Only two competitors from a sub-district or a revenue district can take part in an item at the next higher level, on merit.","Before a court or an appeal authority gives an order on a request of a competitor, it must be made sure that only two competitors take part at the higher level and that the third competitor does not come. If a court or an appeal authority allows a third competitor by a clear order, the performance of the competitors who already qualified is evaluated again, one is left out, and only two from one place take part in an item at the higher level (High Court judgment W.A. No. 1178/2017 dated 22.6.2017).","Each school at the sub-district fair brings an escorting teacher. If girls take part, a woman teacher must go with the team as an escorting teacher. The teachers carry the full responsibility for the children.","SocSci LP is for children of classes 1 to 4, SocSci UP for classes 5 to 7, SocSci HS for classes 8 to 10 and SocSci HSS/VHSS for classes 11 and 12.","A child can take part in only one item of the Social Science Fair.","For the Collection or Charts item, one of the two is chosen at the school. Two children take part as a team.","The Album Making item is for the LP section only, as a team of 2 children.","The Atlas Making, Local History Writing, News Reading, History Seminar and Talent Search Examination are for the HS and HSS/VHSS sections only. The Elocution is for UP, HS and HSS/VHSS.","The Quiz is open to the children of all four sections, one child from each school.","Only teachers who teach the social science subject can take part in the Teaching Aid competition."]},{"Key":"registration","Title":"How to Register","Items":["All competitions from the sub-district level to the state level, including the club level competitions, are held on the online system. The head master or principal makes the online entry of the children with great care.","The photo of every child must be uploaded at the time of registration.","The full responsibility for the online entries is with the head master or principal.","After the sub-district competitions, the list of the competitors chosen for the revenue district competitions is registered online.","The state fair registration committee registers the teams that come from the different districts, gives the registration cards and passes their details to the accommodation committee. It also gives the food coupons to the committees and the team managers.","At the state fair, each competitor gives a short description of the exhibit, with photos, at the time of the competition.","Competitors must come to the place of the competition on the time fixed. A competitor who does not come on time can be made ineligible.","The rules and conditions of each competition item are announced on the stage before the competition starts.","The programme committee gives the code numbers for the competitors from the office of the Director of General Education.","The entry forms of the manual give the details of the school, the competitors and the escorting teachers. The sub-district entry form carries the school name and school (Sampoorna) code, the type of school (Government, Aided or Unaided), the number of boys, girls and others, and the name, designation, phone number and signature of the escorting teacher, with the signature of the head of the institution. The head of the school also signs a declaration that the details agree with the school records and with the manual.","The revenue district and state entry forms carry the name of the sub-district or revenue district, the number of boys and girls, the name of each item, the name of each child in capital letters, the name of the school, the class, the sex and remarks. The state fair form also names the team manager and the escorting teachers with their addresses and carries the signature and seal of the Deputy Director of Education."]},{"Key":"fees","Title":"Fees and Payment","Items":["A registration fee of {{SubDistrictRegistrationFee}} can be collected at the sub-district fair.","The funds for the sub-district fair come from the scheme fund, the special fee fund, public donations, the funds of the science, maths, social science, work experience and IT clubs, and PTA donations.","The funds are spent as per the budget that the organising committee approves. The accounts of the fair are presented to the concerned committee and passed within one month after the fair.","The income and expenses of the funds that the scheme gives are approved by the organising committee within one month after the fair, and then given to the treasury of the education officer.","At state level, the Finance Committee gives the budget of all committees to the organising committee for approval, allows unexpected expenses in advance (to be approved later), and helps the General Convener to prepare the accounts for audit. The audited accounts are approved by the organising committee within one month after the fair.","The committees can take gifts and donations of articles as the organising committee approves. A special register is kept for this and the General Convener signs it.","An appeal costs a fee that depends on the level. There is no appeal fee at the school level (see Complaints and Appeals).","The fee for a new certificate is given under Prizes and Certificates."]},{"Key":"levels","Title":"Levels and Qualification","Items":["The Sasthrolsavam has four levels: school, sub-district, revenue district and state.","School level: the fair is held in one day under the science, maths, social science, work experience and IT clubs of the school. Every child of the school gets the chance to take part by category.","The school fair committee has the ward member as chairman, the PTA president or the SMC chairman as vice chairman, the principal or the first teacher as General Convener, the first teacher or the senior assistant as joint General Convener, the club coordinators as joint convenors, and all teachers, PTA and SMC members, mother PTA members and the school leader as members. Sub-committees can be formed.","Sub-district level (LP, UP, HS, HSS and VHSS): the fair is held in two days. The sub-district education officer, the RDD or AD of Higher Secondary and Vocational Higher Secondary, and the club secretaries choose a suitable school together. The sub-district clubs hold the fair. There are separate competitions for LP, UP, HS and HSS/VHSS, and HSS and VHSS are one unit.","Sub-district winners get certificates and encouragement awards.","The sub-district organising committee has the MPs, MLAs, and the presidents of district, block and grama panchayats, the mayor and the municipal chairmen as patrons; the mayor, municipal chairman or grama panchayat president as chairman; the principal or head master of the venue school as General Convener; the club secretaries as convenors; and the sub-district education officer as treasurer.","Revenue district level (HS, HSS and VHSS): the committees are formed by the Deputy Directors of Education. The fair is held in two days. The Deputy Director of Education, the RDD, the AD and the club secretaries, with the Kite district coordinator, choose suitable schools together. All sub-districts of the revenue district must take part.","At the revenue district fair, HS and HSS/VHSS are evaluated separately. The evaluation records are kept for one year in the office of the Deputy Director of Education. Winners get certificates and encouragement awards.","The revenue district organising committee has the ministers, MPs, MLAs, district panchayat president, mayor, municipal chairmen, District Collector and District Police Chief as patrons; the local MLA as chairman; the Deputy Director of Education as General Convener; and the District Educational Officer of the education district as treasurer.","State level (HS, HSS and VHSS): the fair is held in three days under the General Education Department and the state science, maths, social science, work experience and IT association or council. The Director of General Education decides the place of the fair. All revenue districts of the state must take part.","At the state fair the first three places in each item get certificates, cash awards and honours.","The state organising committee has the Governor, the Chief Minister, the Leader of the Opposition, the Minister of General Education and the ministers of the host district as chief patrons. The Director of General Education is the General Coordinator and the Additional Director of General Education (Academic) is the General Convener.","The state organising (co-ordination) committee is fully responsible for the state fair. It prepares the budget, approves the sums for the sub-committees, approves the income and expense plan of the finance committee, and joins all sub-committees to work together.","The state sub-committees are the Programme, Reception, Exhibition and Cultural, Food, Pandal and Stage, Finance, Publicity, Law and Order, Welfare, Registration, Accommodation, Transport, Trophy, Light and Sound, Souvenir and Green Protocol committees.","The Social Science Fair has these items at the school level: LP has Collection or Charts, Album Making and Quiz; UP has Working Model, Still Model, Elocution and Quiz; HS has Atlas Making, Working Model, Still Model, Local History Writing, Elocution, History Seminar and Quiz; HSS/VHSS has the same seven items as HS.","At the state level, the first, second and third place winners in each item get a certificate and a cash award."]},{"Key":"teams","Title":"Teams, Team Managers and Escorting Teachers","Items":["Each school at the sub-district fair brings an escorting teacher with its team. If girls take part, a woman teacher must go with the team as an escorting teacher.","At the revenue district fair, the team manager of each sub-district team is the sub-district education officer, and the joint team manager is the sub-district secretary of the club association.","At the state fair, each revenue district team has a team manager. Each section has escorting teachers. The secretaries of the district associations are joint team managers. Women teachers go with the girls."]},{"Key":"points","Title":"Points and Grading","Items":["A competitor who gets 60 percent marks or more in an item gets a grade. Grade A is 80 percent or more and gives 5 points. Grade B is 70 percent to 79 percent and gives 3 points. Grade C is 60 percent to 69 percent and gives 1 point.","Items that get less than 60 percent marks are not graded.","Place points: the first place gets 5 points, the second place gets 3 points and the third place gets 1 point.","For the overall championship, the total of the grade points and the place points is counted. If the totals are equal, the number of first places is counted.","Sub-district level: the best school in each of LP, UP, HS and HSS/VHSS is found from the total points of all the fairs. The schools with the best show in each of the Science, Maths, Social Science, Work Experience and IT fairs are also found.","Revenue district level: the best sub-districts and the best schools are found. The championship of HS and of HSS/VHSS is decided from the total points of the five fairs. The schools and sub-districts with the best show in each fair are also found.","State level: the best districts, sub-districts and schools are found. The championship of HS and of HSS/VHSS is decided from the total points of the five fairs. The districts and schools with the best show in each fair are also found.","Only the competition items that are part of a fair count for the championship of that fair.","In the Social Science Fair, the points of the Talent Search Examination and the News Reading competition are not counted for the championship."]},{"Key":"prizes","Title":"Prizes and Certificates","Items":["Competitors who get a grade receive certificates from the school level.","Winners at the sub-district and revenue district fairs get certificates and encouragement awards.","At the state fair, the first three places in each item get certificates, cash awards and honours.","At the closing ceremony of the state fair, the overall championships are given with their certificates and special honours.","The trophy committee collects the trophies of the last year, repairs and polishes them, and prepares the trophies and certificates from the list that the programme committee gives every day. They are given on the same days. Trophies and certificates not given are counted, entered in the trophy register and given to the concerned Deputy Director of Education.","The team manager or the escorting teacher signs the trophy register as clear proof of receiving the trophies.","When the competitions are over, the trophy register is completed and handed to the concerned education officer. At the state level it is handed to the PRO.","Everrolling trophies and shields last for ten years. After that, if the donors do not give new ones, the Director of General Education takes them back.","If a certificate given by the Director of General Education is lost or has errors, the head master or principal sends an application with a demand draft of {{CertificateReplacementFee}} in the name of the Director of General Education to get a new certificate."]},{"Key":"conduct","Title":"Conduct and Safety","Items":["The whole Kerala School Sasthrolsavam must be held strictly under the Green Protocol.","A student, teacher or other person who tries to spoil the Sasthrolsavam by shameless, undisciplined or provoking behaviour gets a proper punishment, including being made ineligible. Action by the department can also be taken.","Only items that keep the safety of the children can be held, from school level to state level. The organisers must take special care to arrange all safety measures.","The Law and Order Committee keeps discipline and order at the venue of the fair, with the help of police officers, SPC, NCC, scouts, guides, other helpful organisations and leading citizens. Police service must be there until the fair ends.","Light is arranged at all places at all times. Plug points are arranged on the stages and stalls (at least 5 power plugs in each room of the exhibition stalls), with a generator and a sound system. The certificates for electrical safety are given to the General Convener 2 days before the fair starts.","The Public Works Department gives the fitness certificate of the stages, and it goes to the General Convener 2 days before the fair starts. The fire force is arranged and a pandal is given for it.","The Welfare Committee looks after the welfare and health of the participants and officials. Medical help and first aid are available at all times with the Public Health Department. Cleaning of the place of stay, the competition places, the food halls and the surroundings is arranged with the local self government department. Drinking water must be there everywhere.","The Green Protocol Committee has the Suchitwa Mission district coordinator as its chairman, gives directions to keep the stages and the surroundings clean and green, gives awareness of the Green Protocol to the competitors and the viewers, and gives guidelines to avoid plastic."]},{"Key":"judging","Title":"Judges and Judging","Items":["Judges are fair, well known and capable persons. When judges are chosen, their bio data and declaration are taken in writing, as in the annexure. At every level at least three judges must be there to judge.","A person cannot be a judge in the science fairs and the related competitions for more than two years. A sub-district judge cannot be a judge at the revenue district fair of the same revenue district. A district level judge cannot be a judge at the state Sasthrolsavam or its related competitions in the same year.","Every year the Director of General Education chooses the state and district judges from the nominations of persons from the Government institutions of the state, the Government recognised education institutions, experts in the subjects and persons in higher education, after a careful check. The Deputy Directors of Education are responsible for choosing the sub-district judges by the same standard.","The programme committee gives the judges the score sheets and the remark sheets in time, and collects and keeps their bio data and declaration.","The score sheet with the evaluation indicators gets the approval of the General Convener.","There must be no cutting or correction on a score sheet. If a correction cannot be avoided, the person who evaluated must sign it fully at the place.","A judge gives an evaluation note, with an opinion, for each child along with the score sheet.","After each competition the score sheet, the remark sheet, the tabulation sheet and the report of the stage manager are collected. The points are written on the score board in time.","The mark lists of the competitions and the tabulation sheets of the results are sealed and kept for one year in the office of the sub-district education officer (or the Deputy Director of Education at revenue district level). They are produced when the appeal committee or higher officers ask for them.","The programme committee prepares the results and uploads them, and gives the results to the trophy committee and the publicity committee in time. It prepares, with the Kite, the list of winners for the higher level competitions and gives it to the upper levels.","All item competitions are recorded on video, and a CD is prepared to help the appeal committee study the complaints. For on-the-spot competitions the video is recorded when the products are finished. The programme committee is responsible for this."]},{"Key":"complaints","Title":"Complaints and Appeals","Items":["An appeal committee must be formed at every level to give judgment on complaints about the results of the Science, Maths, Social Science, Work Experience and IT fairs.","The appeal committees consider appeals on the basis of the judgments of the Honourable High Court on this matter and the rules in the manual. The decision of the appeal committee is final.","A complaint must be given to the appeal committee within one hour after the result is announced. An appeal given after one hour is not accepted for any reason.","The appeal committee studies the complaints clearly, gives its decision and issues the order of action on the appeal in the format of the annexure.","There is no appeal fee at the school level. The appeal fee is {{AppealFeeSubDistrict}} at the sub-district level, {{AppealFeeRevenueDistrict}} at the revenue district level, {{AppealFeeState}} at the state level and {{AppealFeeHigher}} for a higher appeal.","If the decision is in favour of the appeal, the appeal fee is given back. If not, the amount is added to the fund of the organising committee.","The school level appeal committee has the principal or head master and the coordinators of the school science, maths, social science, work experience and IT clubs (one representative of each). Only the competitor who got first place with an A grade at the school level can go to the sub-district fair. The appeal committee confirms this at the time of the appeal check.","The sub-district appeal committee has the District Education Officer as chairman, the sub-district education officer as vice chairman, one representative each of the RDD and the AD, the General Convener and one expert in the subject. Complaints go through the escorting teacher, in the fixed form, with the fixed fee, within one hour after the result is announced. Only complaints given through the escorting teacher are considered.","The revenue district appeal committee has the Deputy Director of Education as chairman, the RDD or AD as vice chairman, the District Education Officer, the Kite district coordinator and an expert or the regional foreman in the subject. Complaints go through the team manager, in the fixed form, with the fixed fee. Only complaints given through the team manager are considered.","The state appeal committee has the Director of General Education as chairman, the Additional Director (Academic) as vice chairman, the Director of Higher Secondary Education, the Director of Vocational Higher Secondary Education, the vice chairman and executive director of Kite, and an expert in the subject. Complaints go through the revenue district team manager or joint team manager, in the fixed form with the fixed fee. Only complaints given through the team manager are considered.","A competitor who goes to a higher level because of a court order or an order of an appeal authority is counted as a participant.","The members of the appeal committee and the judges give a declaration in the format of the annexure."]},{"Key":"other","Title":"Other Parts of the Manual","Items":["This page covers the Social Science Fair only. The Sasthrolsavam also has the Science Fair, Maths Fair, Work Experience Fair, IT Fair. Each has its own page.","The manual also holds the Kerala School Skill Festival for the students of the Vocational Higher Secondary courses, with its own manual part. It is held along with the Sasthrolsavam and is not covered here.","The manual has annexures: the entry forms for each fair and level, the evaluation sheets, a regional list of VHS schools, the format of the identity card for a child, the declaration for judges and appeal committee members, the application form for an appeal and the format of the appeal order.","The Programme Committee of the state fair keeps the competitor lists, score sheets, time sheets, tabulation sheets and score boards, gives the code numbers and a leaflet of instructions, and appoints and names teachers for the smooth running of the competitions. It also gives notices on the stages and keeps the time of every competition as planned. It sees to the internet and wi-fi, computers and equipment at each place, and the printing of certificates and the uploading of results.","The Reception Committee makes the list of persons to be invited to the opening and the closing, invites the guests, prints and gives invitations with the approval of the protocol officer, and receives the guests, the judges and the teams that come from the districts.","The Exhibition and Cultural Committee makes all the facilities for the exhibition (with the evaluation committee) at the venue school, keeps the exhibits from the districts safe, arranges facilities for judges to evaluate at the stalls, and lets the students and the public visit all stalls. It also gives code numbers and stall numbers for each district and arranges cultural programmes after the exhibition time.","The Food Committee gives food to the students, teachers, guests, judges, officials, organising committee members, volunteers and media persons. On the evaluation day food is given at the stalls. The special food items needed by the competitors of the on-the-spot competitions must be given at the competition place.","The Pandal and Stage Committee builds the pandals for the on-the-spot competitions and the exhibition stalls, with separate stalls for each section of each district, and separate stalls for the Primary, HS, HSS and VHSS teaching aids and teacher projects, for the Work Experience on-the-spot competitions and for the exhibition. It also builds the pandals for the opening and the closing, with toilets and urinals, chairs and furniture.","The Publicity Committee gives publicity for all the matters of the Sasthrolsavam in audio, visual and print media, banners and arches, and makes sure the competition results from the Programme Committee reach the news media in time.","The Accommodation Committee decides the place of stay for the teams that have completed registration, and arranges water, light, bathrooms and toilets. The stay of the judges, the officials and the committee convenors at the state fair is kept with the Public Relations Officer of the Directorate of General Education.","The Transport Committee arranges vehicles for the teams and guests that come to the fair, and asks the railway, the State Transport, the private bus owners and the school buses for special services to the venue and back.","The Souvenir Committee prepares and publishes the Sasthrolsavam Souvenir (Sasthrolsava Rekha). It collects articles, photos and advertisements with the approval of the General Convener, takes advertisement amounts at the tariff rate, bears the cost of the publication, gives the Souvenir with details and pictures to the General Convener within one month after the fair, and sends copies to the District Education Offices and the Higher Secondary and Vocational Higher Secondary Directorates.","The Teaching Aid competition for social science teachers (Primary, HS and HSS/VHSS sections) is in this chapter of the manual but is not a student competition. It is an on-the-spot competition of 3 hours. The teacher makes a teaching aid on the lessons that can be used to exchange the lesson in the class room. It can only be made by teachers who teach social science.","The Teaching Aid is not counted for the championship of the fair. The score table is not printed in the manual."]}],"Documents":[{"Title":"Kerala School Sasthrolsavam Manual 2025 (PDF)","Url":"https://mela.kite.kerala.gov.in/2026/entry_forms/sasthrolsavam_manual_2025.pdf"},{"Title":"Kerala School Sasthrolsavam Item List 2026 (KITE)","Url":"https://mela.kite.kerala.gov.in/2026/index.php/Home/item_list"}]}'),i=[{ParticipantType:"Team",Source:"Manual 2025 Ch.8 A, pp.93-94",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Two children take part as a team in the Collections, Charts and Album Making items of the LP section, and in the Working Model and Still Model items of the UP, HS and HSS/VHSS sections."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Two children take part as a team in the Collections, Charts and Album Making items of the LP section, and in the Working Model and Still Model items of the UP, HS and HSS/VHSS sections.`},{ParticipantType:"Individual",Source:"Manual 2025 Ch.8 A, pp.93-94",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["One child takes part in the Social Science Quiz, Elocution, Atlas Making, Local History Writing, History Seminar, News Reading and Talent Search Examination."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- One child takes part in the Social Science Quiz, Elocution, Atlas Making, Local History Writing, History Seminar, News Reading and Talent Search Examination.`}],n=[{AgeGroup:"SocSci LP",Source:"Manual 2025 Ch.8 A 1, p.93",Sections:{About:["The LP section is for children of classes 1 to 4."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["There are three items: Collection or Charts (on the spot), Album Making (on the spot) and Quiz.","Two children take part as a team in Collection or Charts and in Album Making. In the quiz one child from the school takes part.","Every competition of this section ends at the sub-district level."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- The LP section is for children of classes 1 to 4.

## Rules
- There are three items: Collection or Charts (on the spot), Album Making (on the spot) and Quiz.
- Two children take part as a team in Collection or Charts and in Album Making. In the quiz one child from the school takes part.
- Every competition of this section ends at the sub-district level.`},{AgeGroup:"SocSci UP",Source:"Manual 2025 Ch.8 A 2, p.93",Sections:{About:["The UP section is for children of classes 5 to 7."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["There are four items: Working Model (2 children, on the spot), Still Model (2 children, on the spot), Elocution (1 child) and Quiz (1 child).","Every competition of this section ends at the sub-district level."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- The UP section is for children of classes 5 to 7.

## Rules
- There are four items: Working Model (2 children, on the spot), Still Model (2 children, on the spot), Elocution (1 child) and Quiz (1 child).
- Every competition of this section ends at the sub-district level.`},{AgeGroup:"SocSci HS",Source:"Manual 2025 Ch.8 A 3, p.94",Sections:{About:["The HS section is for children of classes 8 to 10."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["There are seven items: Atlas Making (1 child), Working Model (2 children), Still Model (2 children), Local History Writing (1 child), Elocution (1 child), History Seminar (1 child) and Quiz (1 child). All are on the spot except the quiz.","The Talent Search Examination and the News Reading are also held for this section."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- The HS section is for children of classes 8 to 10.

## Rules
- There are seven items: Atlas Making (1 child), Working Model (2 children), Still Model (2 children), Local History Writing (1 child), Elocution (1 child), History Seminar (1 child) and Quiz (1 child). All are on the spot except the quiz.
- The Talent Search Examination and the News Reading are also held for this section.`},{AgeGroup:"SocSci HSS/VHSS",Source:"Manual 2025 Ch.8 A 4, p.94",Sections:{About:["The HSS/VHSS section is for children of classes 11 and 12 of Higher Secondary and Vocational Higher Secondary."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["There are seven items: Atlas Making (1 child), Working Model (2 children), Still Model (2 children), Local History Writing (1 child), Elocution (1 child), History Seminar (1 child) and Quiz (1 child). All are on the spot except the quiz.","The Talent Search Examination and the News Reading are also held for this section."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- The HSS/VHSS section is for children of classes 11 and 12 of Higher Secondary and Vocational Higher Secondary.

## Rules
- There are seven items: Atlas Making (1 child), Working Model (2 children), Still Model (2 children), Local History Writing (1 child), Elocution (1 child), History Seminar (1 child) and Quiz (1 child). All are on the spot except the quiz.
- The Talent Search Examination and the News Reading are also held for this section.`}],o=JSON.parse(`[{"Title":"Collections","Source":"Manual 2025 Ch.8 B 1, p.95 and E 1, p.118","Sections":{"About":["This is an on-the-spot item of the LP section. Collection or Charts is chosen at the school. Two children take part as a team."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["The collection can be of old things, stamps, stones, soils, fossils, coins and currency notes, or any other collection that is linked to the study of social science.","When the collections are shown, notes that help to understand them must be written and shown along with each collection."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Diversity: 30 marks.","Collection method and the part of the children: 20 marks.","Relation with the curriculum: 20 marks.","Organisation: 20 marks.","Explanation or interview: 10 marks.","The total is 100 marks."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- This is an on-the-spot item of the LP section. Collection or Charts is chosen at the school. Two children take part as a team.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- The collection can be of old things, stamps, stones, soils, fossils, coins and currency notes, or any other collection that is linked to the study of social science.\\n- When the collections are shown, notes that help to understand them must be written and shown along with each collection.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Diversity: 30 marks.\\n- Collection method and the part of the children: 20 marks.\\n- Relation with the curriculum: 20 marks.\\n- Organisation: 20 marks.\\n- Explanation or interview: 10 marks.\\n- The total is 100 marks."},{"Title":"Charts","Source":"Manual 2025 Ch.8 B 1, pp.95-96 and E 4, p.119","Sections":{"About":["This is an on-the-spot item of the LP section. Charts is chosen at the school in place of Collection. Two children take part as a team."],"TimeAndFormat":["The longest making time is 3 hours."],"Rules":["The charts are made on the subject given. The subject for the charts is fixed each year by the State Social Science Club Association and told to the schools beforehand.","A team makes at most 6 charts.","The competitors bring the chart papers and other materials for making the charts.","The ideas of the given subject are put in the charts in order.","The charts are written so that a clear idea can be gained from them.","The charts are made so that they can be used in the class room as a learning help.","Cuttings from periodicals must not be used in the charts."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Subject relevance and ideas: 30 marks.","Diversity: 20 marks.","Creativity and organisation: 20 marks.","Attractiveness: 20 marks.","Explanation or interview: 10 marks.","The total is 100 marks."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- This is an on-the-spot item of the LP section. Charts is chosen at the school in place of Collection. Two children take part as a team.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- The longest making time is 3 hours.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- The charts are made on the subject given. The subject for the charts is fixed each year by the State Social Science Club Association and told to the schools beforehand.\\n- A team makes at most 6 charts.\\n- The competitors bring the chart papers and other materials for making the charts.\\n- The ideas of the given subject are put in the charts in order.\\n- The charts are written so that a clear idea can be gained from them.\\n- The charts are made so that they can be used in the class room as a learning help.\\n- Cuttings from periodicals must not be used in the charts.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Subject relevance and ideas: 30 marks.\\n- Diversity: 20 marks.\\n- Creativity and organisation: 20 marks.\\n- Attractiveness: 20 marks.\\n- Explanation or interview: 10 marks.\\n- The total is 100 marks."},{"Title":"Album Making","Source":"Manual 2025 Ch.8 B 2, pp.96-97 and E 9, p.120","Sections":{"About":["This is an on-the-spot item of the LP section. Two children take part as a team."],"TimeAndFormat":["The longest making time is 3 hours."],"Rules":["The team chooses and makes an album on one of three subjects that the State Social Science Club Association gives.","The children collect pictures from periodicals, cut them and stick them on the A4 or chart paper that the organisers give.","Notes that explain the pictures are written below the pictures stuck on the paper.","The album has an attractive cover page.","At most 10 sheets, including the cover, can be used. Both sides of the paper can be used."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Relation of the pictures with the subject, and ideas: 40 marks.","Captions and arrangement of ideas: 20 marks.","Creativity and novelty: 20 marks.","Variety of pictures, layout, attractiveness and completeness: 20 marks.","The total is 100 marks."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- This is an on-the-spot item of the LP section. Two children take part as a team.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- The longest making time is 3 hours.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- The team chooses and makes an album on one of three subjects that the State Social Science Club Association gives.\\n- The children collect pictures from periodicals, cut them and stick them on the A4 or chart paper that the organisers give.\\n- Notes that explain the pictures are written below the pictures stuck on the paper.\\n- The album has an attractive cover page.\\n- At most 10 sheets, including the cover, can be used. Both sides of the paper can be used.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Relation of the pictures with the subject, and ideas: 40 marks.\\n- Captions and arrangement of ideas: 20 marks.\\n- Creativity and novelty: 20 marks.\\n- Variety of pictures, layout, attractiveness and completeness: 20 marks.\\n- The total is 100 marks."},{"Title":"Social Science Quiz","Source":"Manual 2025 Ch.8 B 3, p.97 and B 7, pp.108, 116","Sections":{"About":["Each school sends one child from the school level to the sub-district level."],"TimeAndFormat":["The quiz is held in Malayalam, with an English translation.","The quiz has 20 questions."],"Rules":["LP: the questions are from history, environment study and general knowledge.","UP: history 6, geography 6 and general knowledge 8 questions.","HS and HSS/VHSS: history 6, geography 6, general knowledge 4, economics 2 and civics 2 questions.","School level quiz: the questions are made by the school, under the responsibility of the teachers with the school social science club.","Sub-district level: the questions are made by the district social science club association with the permission of the Deputy Director of Education, and given to the schools. Modern technology must be used.","Revenue district and state levels: the Department of General Education makes the questions."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Each school sends one child from the school level to the sub-district level.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- The quiz is held in Malayalam, with an English translation.\\n- The quiz has 20 questions.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- LP: the questions are from history, environment study and general knowledge.\\n- UP: history 6, geography 6 and general knowledge 8 questions.\\n- HS and HSS/VHSS: history 6, geography 6, general knowledge 4, economics 2 and civics 2 questions.\\n- School level quiz: the questions are made by the school, under the responsibility of the teachers with the school social science club.\\n- Sub-district level: the questions are made by the district social science club association with the permission of the Deputy Director of Education, and given to the schools. Modern technology must be used.\\n- Revenue district and state levels: the Department of General Education makes the questions."},{"Title":"Working Model","Source":"Manual 2025 Ch.8 B 2.1, B 3.2, B 4.2, pp.97-98, 102, 112","Sections":{"About":["This is held for UP, HS and HSS/VHSS. The model explains an idea of the curriculum."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["This is an on-the-spot competition. The longest making time is 3 hours.","Two children take part as a team.","The model must fit in a space of 2 metres x 2 metres.","The model is made on ideas in the textbook content.","Research possibilities must be used in making and showing the model.","At most 5 charts that relate to the model can be made and shown.","Materials that follow the Green Protocol are used for making."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Relation with the curriculum and the idea: 20 marks.","Construction materials (high cost, low cost, zero cost): 20 marks.","Technical knowledge, construction skill and how well it works: 30 marks.","Novelty, originality and attractiveness: 20 marks.","Explanation and interview: 10 marks.","The total is 100 marks."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- This is held for UP, HS and HSS/VHSS. The model explains an idea of the curriculum.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- This is an on-the-spot competition. The longest making time is 3 hours.\\n- Two children take part as a team.\\n- The model must fit in a space of 2 metres x 2 metres.\\n- The model is made on ideas in the textbook content.\\n- Research possibilities must be used in making and showing the model.\\n- At most 5 charts that relate to the model can be made and shown.\\n- Materials that follow the Green Protocol are used for making.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Relation with the curriculum and the idea: 20 marks.\\n- Construction materials (high cost, low cost, zero cost): 20 marks.\\n- Technical knowledge, construction skill and how well it works: 30 marks.\\n- Novelty, originality and attractiveness: 20 marks.\\n- Explanation and interview: 10 marks.\\n- The total is 100 marks."},{"Title":"Still Model","Source":"Manual 2025 Ch.8 B 2.2, B 3.3, B 4.3, pp.98, 103, 112","Sections":{"About":["This is held for UP, HS and HSS/VHSS."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["This is an on-the-spot competition. The longest making time is 3 hours.","Two children take part as a team.","The model must fit in a space of 2 metres x 2 metres.","The model is made on ideas in the textbook content.","Research possibilities must be used in making and showing the model.","At most 5 charts that relate to the model can be made and shown.","Materials that follow the Green Protocol are used for making."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Relation with the curriculum and the idea: 20 marks.","Construction materials (high cost, low cost, zero cost): 20 marks.","Technical knowledge, construction skill and how well it works: 30 marks.","Novelty, originality and attractiveness: 20 marks.","Explanation and interview: 10 marks.","The total is 100 marks."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- This is held for UP, HS and HSS/VHSS.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- This is an on-the-spot competition. The longest making time is 3 hours.\\n- Two children take part as a team.\\n- The model must fit in a space of 2 metres x 2 metres.\\n- The model is made on ideas in the textbook content.\\n- Research possibilities must be used in making and showing the model.\\n- At most 5 charts that relate to the model can be made and shown.\\n- Materials that follow the Green Protocol are used for making.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Relation with the curriculum and the idea: 20 marks.\\n- Construction materials (high cost, low cost, zero cost): 20 marks.\\n- Technical knowledge, construction skill and how well it works: 30 marks.\\n- Novelty, originality and attractiveness: 20 marks.\\n- Explanation and interview: 10 marks.\\n- The total is 100 marks."},{"Title":"Elocution","Source":"Manual 2025 Ch.8 B 2.3, B 3.5, B 4.5, pp.99, 106, 115","Sections":{"About":["This is held for UP, HS and HSS/VHSS. One child takes part."],"TimeAndFormat":["The speech takes 5 minutes."],"Rules":["The speech must be in Malayalam.","The judges decide the subject and give it to the competitor 5 minutes before the competition."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Content and idea: 30 marks.","Language purity: 20 marks.","Clarity of diction: 20 marks.","Fluency: 20 marks.","Word organisation: 10 marks.","The total is 100 marks."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- This is held for UP, HS and HSS/VHSS. One child takes part.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- The speech takes 5 minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- The speech must be in Malayalam.\\n- The judges decide the subject and give it to the competitor 5 minutes before the competition.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Content and idea: 30 marks.\\n- Language purity: 20 marks.\\n- Clarity of diction: 20 marks.\\n- Fluency: 20 marks.\\n- Word organisation: 10 marks.\\n- The total is 100 marks."},{"Title":"Atlas Making","Source":"Manual 2025 Ch.8 B 3.1, B 4.1, pp.100-102 and E 7, p.120","Sections":{"About":["This is an on-the-spot item of the HS and HSS/VHSS sections. One child takes part."],"TimeAndFormat":["The longest time is 3 hours."],"Rules":["The organisers give an A4 outline map of India and a tracing paper. The child can draw the needed maps with the tracing paper.","The locations are marked on the map the child draws.","Water colours, colour pencils and crayons can be used to mark. Ink pen, ball point pen, gel pen and micro tip pen can be used to write the borders and the names of places.","The judges give 10 subjects, and the competitor draws 7 maps from them. 2 of the 7 maps must be drawn by all the competitors: the political map of India (mark the states, the union territories and their capitals) and the physical map of India (mark the natural regions of India).","The atlas has an attractive cover. After the cover page there is a page with the contents.","Grid, suitable colours, signs, directions, scale, indicators and clear marking must be in the maps.","The maps the child draws must be stitched in order.","Of the 10 questions given to decide the places on the maps, some are application type questions.","The outline map and the tracing paper are given back to the invigilator after the competition. The child can leave the hall only after giving them back.","The maps of the atlas are drawn only on the sealed papers that the organisers give. The competitor must not bring any papers from outside."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Idea link, precision and accuracy: 40 marks.","Outline, precision and beauty: 20 marks.","Indicators, colour, grid, signs and so on: 20 marks.","Cover page: 10 marks.","Attractiveness: 10 marks.","The total is 100 marks."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- This is an on-the-spot item of the HS and HSS/VHSS sections. One child takes part.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- The longest time is 3 hours.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- The organisers give an A4 outline map of India and a tracing paper. The child can draw the needed maps with the tracing paper.\\n- The locations are marked on the map the child draws.\\n- Water colours, colour pencils and crayons can be used to mark. Ink pen, ball point pen, gel pen and micro tip pen can be used to write the borders and the names of places.\\n- The judges give 10 subjects, and the competitor draws 7 maps from them. 2 of the 7 maps must be drawn by all the competitors: the political map of India (mark the states, the union territories and their capitals) and the physical map of India (mark the natural regions of India).\\n- The atlas has an attractive cover. After the cover page there is a page with the contents.\\n- Grid, suitable colours, signs, directions, scale, indicators and clear marking must be in the maps.\\n- The maps the child draws must be stitched in order.\\n- Of the 10 questions given to decide the places on the maps, some are application type questions.\\n- The outline map and the tracing paper are given back to the invigilator after the competition. The child can leave the hall only after giving them back.\\n- The maps of the atlas are drawn only on the sealed papers that the organisers give. The competitor must not bring any papers from outside.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Idea link, precision and accuracy: 40 marks.\\n- Outline, precision and beauty: 20 marks.\\n- Indicators, colour, grid, signs and so on: 20 marks.\\n- Cover page: 10 marks.\\n- Attractiveness: 10 marks.\\n- The total is 100 marks."},{"Title":"Local History Writing","Source":"Manual 2025 Ch.8 B 3.4, B 4.4, pp.103-106 and E 6, p.119","Sections":{"About":["This is an on-the-spot item of the HS and HSS/VHSS sections. One child takes part."],"TimeAndFormat":["The longest time is 3 hours.","The interview takes 5 minutes for each competitor. Very relevant documents can be shown to the judges at the time of the interview. A digital presentation is not needed in the interview."],"Rules":["The local history is written in Malayalam.","The child writes the local history of an area that is part of Kerala.","The writing is based on the subjects that the State Social Science Clubs Association gives each year. The subjects are given at the state level; the school, sub-district, revenue district and state level competitions use them.","At each level 4 subjects are given. The competitor chooses one by lot at the time of the competition. At the state level, the subject is given only at the competition time.","This is a method of writing minute history, so the details related to the subject are found from as many sources as possible and presented accurately.","The writing should include: the history of the name of the place (of the chosen area), geographical features, people and life, history of development (the findings on the subject given are presented in detail), driving forces (persons, movements, organisations, events that brought changes), foreign relations (relations with neighbouring and far places), historical remains (related to the subject), and events (works of persons, movements and organisations that gave events to modern society related to the subject).","The competitors do not bring any notes or photos at the time of the competition. The writing is done on the sealed papers that the organisers give.","The competitors must follow the rules of the manual for the local history writing."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Writing skill and arrangement of ideas: 30 marks.","Language style: 20 marks.","Method of collecting information and the part of the child: 10 marks.","Finding fine details and presentation: 20 marks.","Diversity of content: 10 marks.","Interview: 10 marks.","The total is 100 marks."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- This is an on-the-spot item of the HS and HSS/VHSS sections. One child takes part.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- The longest time is 3 hours.\\n- The interview takes 5 minutes for each competitor. Very relevant documents can be shown to the judges at the time of the interview. A digital presentation is not needed in the interview.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- The local history is written in Malayalam.\\n- The child writes the local history of an area that is part of Kerala.\\n- The writing is based on the subjects that the State Social Science Clubs Association gives each year. The subjects are given at the state level; the school, sub-district, revenue district and state level competitions use them.\\n- At each level 4 subjects are given. The competitor chooses one by lot at the time of the competition. At the state level, the subject is given only at the competition time.\\n- This is a method of writing minute history, so the details related to the subject are found from as many sources as possible and presented accurately.\\n- The writing should include: the history of the name of the place (of the chosen area), geographical features, people and life, history of development (the findings on the subject given are presented in detail), driving forces (persons, movements, organisations, events that brought changes), foreign relations (relations with neighbouring and far places), historical remains (related to the subject), and events (works of persons, movements and organisations that gave events to modern society related to the subject).\\n- The competitors do not bring any notes or photos at the time of the competition. The writing is done on the sealed papers that the organisers give.\\n- The competitors must follow the rules of the manual for the local history writing.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Writing skill and arrangement of ideas: 30 marks.\\n- Language style: 20 marks.\\n- Method of collecting information and the part of the child: 10 marks.\\n- Finding fine details and presentation: 20 marks.\\n- Diversity of content: 10 marks.\\n- Interview: 10 marks.\\n- The total is 100 marks."},{"Title":"History Seminar","Source":"Manual 2025 Ch.8 B 3.6, B 4.6, pp.107-108 and E 10, pp.120-121","Sections":{"About":["This is an on-the-spot item of the HS and HSS/VHSS sections. One child takes part. It is called Charithra Seminar (History Seminar)."],"TimeAndFormat":["Reference time is 1 hour and the seminar paper writing 2 hours. The presentation takes 5 minutes. The viva is followed by a discussion of 5 minutes."],"Rules":["The state social science clubs association gives the seminar paper subjects and tells the schools. On the day of the paper writing competition the children write a seminar paper on one subject drawn by lot from these subjects.","The paper is based on research, so the children can bring reference materials into the hall for the subject they get. The invigilator checks these references before the writing starts. The paper is finished in 3 hours (1 hour for reference and 2 hours for writing).","The competition is finished in 2 days. On the first day the paper is written. On the second day it is presented.","After the seminar paper is evaluated, the paper is handed to the child half an hour before the presentation on the presentation day, for preparing the presentation. All papers are then taken back. 5 minutes before the presentation the paper is given to the child, and after the presentation it is taken back.","The presentation takes 5 minutes. The judges then hold a viva, and 5 minutes are given for discussion.","The winner is decided from the score of the seminar paper and the score of the presentation.","At the state level the seminar is held in 2 days. The competitor is chosen from the school level and moves up to the higher level."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Seminar paper: 40 marks. The criteria are the study, observation, research, analysis and conclusion; ideas; arrangement of the content; language style and ability to exchange; and the formal and creative nature.","Presentation: 40 marks. The criteria are language purity and clearness of the idea, style and novelty of presentation, the ability to present without stage fear, the grasp of the subject and the feeling with the audience.","Discussion: 10 marks. The criteria are the part in the discussion, the ability to answer, the ability to support with reasons and the grasp of the subject.","The seminar presentation aids are marked out of 10: the variety of visual and audio exchange tools, attractiveness and quality, novelty and accurate use."],"Points":null,"Notes":["The three marked sections (40, 40 and 10) add up to 90, not 100. The manual prints the 10 marks of the presentation aids separately, but not as a part of the total of 100. The manual does not say how the two fit together."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- This is an on-the-spot item of the HS and HSS/VHSS sections. One child takes part. It is called Charithra Seminar (History Seminar).\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Reference time is 1 hour and the seminar paper writing 2 hours. The presentation takes 5 minutes. The viva is followed by a discussion of 5 minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- The state social science clubs association gives the seminar paper subjects and tells the schools. On the day of the paper writing competition the children write a seminar paper on one subject drawn by lot from these subjects.\\n- The paper is based on research, so the children can bring reference materials into the hall for the subject they get. The invigilator checks these references before the writing starts. The paper is finished in 3 hours (1 hour for reference and 2 hours for writing).\\n- The competition is finished in 2 days. On the first day the paper is written. On the second day it is presented.\\n- After the seminar paper is evaluated, the paper is handed to the child half an hour before the presentation on the presentation day, for preparing the presentation. All papers are then taken back. 5 minutes before the presentation the paper is given to the child, and after the presentation it is taken back.\\n- The presentation takes 5 minutes. The judges then hold a viva, and 5 minutes are given for discussion.\\n- The winner is decided from the score of the seminar paper and the score of the presentation.\\n- At the state level the seminar is held in 2 days. The competitor is chosen from the school level and moves up to the higher level.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Seminar paper: 40 marks. The criteria are the study, observation, research, analysis and conclusion; ideas; arrangement of the content; language style and ability to exchange; and the formal and creative nature.\\n- Presentation: 40 marks. The criteria are language purity and clearness of the idea, style and novelty of presentation, the ability to present without stage fear, the grasp of the subject and the feeling with the audience.\\n- Discussion: 10 marks. The criteria are the part in the discussion, the ability to answer, the ability to support with reasons and the grasp of the subject.\\n- The seminar presentation aids are marked out of 10: the variety of visual and audio exchange tools, attractiveness and quality, novelty and accurate use.\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- The three marked sections (40, 40 and 10) add up to 90, not 100. The manual prints the 10 marks of the presentation aids separately, but not as a part of the total of 100. The manual does not say how the two fit together."},{"Title":"News Reading","Source":"Manual 2025 Ch.8 Social Science Talent Search and News Reading, p.117-118 and E 8, p.120","Sections":{"About":["This is held for the HS and HSS/VHSS sections. The school competition is under the control of the head master, through the school social science club. The sub-district competition is under the sub-district education officer, through the sub-district social science club. The revenue district competition is under the Deputy Director of Education, through the revenue district social science club. The state competition is under the Director of General Education, run by the State Social Science Club Association."],"TimeAndFormat":["The competitor edits and writes the news in 30 minutes. The news is presented on the stage in 5 minutes."],"Rules":["Making the news: the competitor is given the news of the same day from three well known Malayalam daily papers, to prepare the news. The news that is to be presented in 5 minutes is edited and written in 30 minutes.","At the same time, a news item that is in all three papers is chosen by the three judges and given to the competitors. The competitor writes a review of that news in the competitor's own view and presents it.","When the editing time is over, the competitors give the scripts they made, with their code numbers, to the officials.","Presentation: 5 minutes before each competitor's presentation, the officials give the script back to the competitor to read and make it clear. Then the competitor presents it in 5 minutes on the stage."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Editing: 40 marks.","Clarity of idea and fluency: 20 marks.","Presentation and originality: 20 marks.","Facial expression: 10 marks.","News review: 10 marks.","The total is 100 marks."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- This is held for the HS and HSS/VHSS sections. The school competition is under the control of the head master, through the school social science club. The sub-district competition is under the sub-district education officer, through the sub-district social science club. The revenue district competition is under the Deputy Director of Education, through the revenue district social science club. The state competition is under the Director of General Education, run by the State Social Science Club Association.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- The competitor edits and writes the news in 30 minutes. The news is presented on the stage in 5 minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Making the news: the competitor is given the news of the same day from three well known Malayalam daily papers, to prepare the news. The news that is to be presented in 5 minutes is edited and written in 30 minutes.\\n- At the same time, a news item that is in all three papers is chosen by the three judges and given to the competitors. The competitor writes a review of that news in the competitor's own view and presents it.\\n- When the editing time is over, the competitors give the scripts they made, with their code numbers, to the officials.\\n- Presentation: 5 minutes before each competitor's presentation, the officials give the script back to the competitor to read and make it clear. Then the competitor presents it in 5 minutes on the stage.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Editing: 40 marks.\\n- Clarity of idea and fluency: 20 marks.\\n- Presentation and originality: 20 marks.\\n- Facial expression: 10 marks.\\n- News review: 10 marks.\\n- The total is 100 marks."},{"Title":"Talent Search Examination","Source":"Manual 2025 Ch.8 Social Science Talent Search, p.117","Sections":{"About":["This is for children of the HS and HSS/VHSS (including Vocational Higher Secondary) sections. The competition finds the talents and skills of children in many ways."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["The question model has 10 questions and their sub-questions. The competition uses many methods: knowledge building, constructive activities, discussions, dialogues, short visual discoveries, script writing, short speeches, slogan writing and poster designing.","At the state level the first, second and third place winners get a certificate and a cash award."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- This is for children of the HS and HSS/VHSS (including Vocational Higher Secondary) sections. The competition finds the talents and skills of children in many ways.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- The question model has 10 questions and their sub-questions. The competition uses many methods: knowledge building, constructive activities, discussions, dialogues, short visual discoveries, script writing, short speeches, slogan writing and poster designing.\\n- At the state level the first, second and third place winners get a certificate and a cash award."}]`),s=[],r=[{Key:"SubDistrictRegistrationFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Registration fee at the sub-district fair",Value:"Rs. 20 per child, collected only from children of classes 9, 10, 11 and 12",Source:"Manual 2025 Ch.2 B 7(b), p.12"},{Key:"AppealFeeSubDistrict",Kind:"fee",Label:"Appeal fee, sub-district level",Value:"Rs. 1000 per appeal",Source:"Manual 2025 Ch.4 A, p.30 and C, p.32"},{Key:"AppealFeeRevenueDistrict",Kind:"fee",Label:"Appeal fee, revenue district level",Value:"Rs. 1500 per appeal",Source:"Manual 2025 Ch.4 A, p.30 and D, p.32"},{Key:"AppealFeeState",Kind:"fee",Label:"Appeal fee, state level",Value:"Rs. 2000 per appeal",Source:"Manual 2025 Ch.4 A, p.30 and E, p.33"},{Key:"AppealFeeHigher",Kind:"fee",Label:"Appeal fee, higher appeal",Value:"Rs. 2500 per appeal",Source:"Manual 2025 Ch.4 A, p.30"},{Key:"CertificateReplacementFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Fee for a new certificate when one is lost or has errors",Value:"Rs. 250 by demand draft in the name of the Director of General Education",Source:"Manual 2025 Ch.1 A 16, p.6"},{Key:"Announcements",Kind:"news",Label:"Order printed with the manual",Value:[{Date:"2025-09-12",Title:"Sasthrolsavam to be held from school level this year",Text:"By order No. DGE/7858/2024 dated 12 September 2025 the Additional Director (Academic) of the Directorate of General Education issued the revised Sasthrolsavam Manual 2025 and gave the direction to hold the Sasthrolsavam from school level this year. The order refers to Government orders (Rt) No. 146/2025/GEDN dated 23 August 2025, No. 118/2024/GEDN dated 20 September 2024 and No. 116/2024/GEDN dated 19 September 2024.",Url:"https://mela.kite.kerala.gov.in/2026/entry_forms/sasthrolsavam_manual_2025.pdf"}],Source:"Manual 2025 order page, PDF p.1"}],h=[{Claim:"Rules for every fair (categories, levels, eligibility, online entry, grades, appeals, championship, committees, judges) and the Social Science Fair chapter (items, rules, score indicators, quiz, atlas, local history, history seminar, news reading, talent search) are taken from the manual. The entry forms and annexures at the end of the manual were read for the entry steps.",SourceType:"Primary - the event's own rules document",Source:"Kerala School Sasthrolsavam Manual 2025, General Education Department, Government of Kerala, Chapters 1-5, Chapter 8 and the annexures",Url:"https://mela.kite.kerala.gov.in/2026/entry_forms/sasthrolsavam_manual_2025.pdf"},{Claim:"The list of items per group (LP, UP, HS, HSS/VHSS) and the item type (team or individual) is the official 2026 item list.",SourceType:"Primary - the event's own rules document",Source:"Kerala School Sasthrolsavam Item List 2026, KITE (Kerala Infrastructure and Technology for Education)",Url:"https://mela.kite.kerala.gov.in/2026/index.php/Home/item_list"}],c={TemplateKey:e,Year:2025,Note:t,Page:a,ParticipantTypes:i,AgeGroups:n,Competitions:o,CompetitionAgeGroups:s,Variables:r,Evidence:h};export{n as AgeGroups,s as CompetitionAgeGroups,o as Competitions,h as Evidence,t as Note,a as Page,i as ParticipantTypes,e as TemplateKey,r as Variables,l as Year,c as default};
